e economy changed in a few  famous british recessions from history  the 90s and the 30s  a dip then a recovery but now look at  these lines  the red one is the recent crisis the  blue one is the american great  depression that began with the 1929 wall  street crash  our recent recession was far less deep  or dramatic than the american 1930s  but look at what happened next the u.s  economy bounced back quickly in the  1930s  dipped slightly and then searched again  while britain simply failed to take off  after this crisis  the upshot is that this year a decade on  from the slump britain's cumulative  growth is now  smaller than america's was 10 years  after the great depression it's a  staggering statistic and perhaps that  explains why  even now ten years on some workers feel  the country  has never truly recovered the still  industry suffered a deeper crisis than  most  today veteran still workers feel they're  constantly  fighting for survival just people worry  you don't know where you're going what  you're going to do you don't know if  you've got a job next week  we lost a lot too many 2008 2009 and you  just don't know what's happening  your life's on old well it's been tough  for my family  in the sense that my son lost his job  he was with me when the political shock  um he's come talk my brother-in-law lost  his job  how much money did they actually  pour into the banks to bail the banks  out the  the answer by the way is 133 billion  pounds or more than a trillion by some  measures  the idea was we'd all benefit but try  telling that to these guys  there's nothing there you take that  steelworks away  you've got a ghost town there's just  nothing  there's that many men out to work so  they decide to sell their houses out  prices for yes to probably can't sell  your house because there's nobody up  there  anybody wants to buy it i always  remember in 2008 we started weathering  the storm  and i still believe that was still  weather in the storm  i don't think that we've ever come out  of it we've never signed see no sunshine  so why was it the crisis the banks or  what happened after  the cuts  [Music]  we must pay down this deficit this  emergency budget deals decisively with  our country's record debts  yes it is tough but it is also fair  if you don't know what the deep cause is  you've got to find solutions  that will help whatever the deep course  is the real cost to the united kingdom  is the economic cost of how they handle  the years that  followed and the obvious thing to do  would have been spending on the  infrastructure  they thought they could eliminate the  deficits in a five-year period which i  think is wildly optimistic  austerity cutting government borrowing  arguably the most controversial economic  policy in recent years  but away from the boardrooms and the  statistics what does it actually  feel like on the ground we get loads of  support  um from the local community local  schools  churches kerry runs a homeless charity  in harlow  offering everything from a warm meal to  help with job applications  the government says well listen we've  got through the worst of it things are  getting better now  do you do you recognize that kind of on  the ground  i don't think we're seeing that just yet  right not here  the average number of homeless people  sleeping on the streets on any night in  2010 was six  um we've just reviewed our figures now  and for our last financial year the  average  number of homeless people on any given  night in harlow is 23.  okay wow okay so it's kind of quite an  increase and that's been increasing  year on year on year not only in harlow  but you know across the uk  a challenge in other words which seems  to be getting worse  not better for those who've had to live  out on the streets  it's hard i mean sometimes i was sitting  there it was so cold in my tent  i was sitting there thinking just about  committing a petty crime to get myself  phoned in prison  like to get out 